WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.820
Your AI answer looks right.

00:02.290 --> 00:07.140
But does it still work when the request
changes, or when an important detail is
missing?

00:07.780 --> 00:11.560
If you can't repeat the check, every
prompt edit becomes guesswork.

00:12.250 --> 00:19.370
ArtemisKit is an open-source toolkit for
evaluating AI behavior against the cases
and expectations you choose.

00:19.900 --> 00:26.000
It gives you a repeatable way to see what
was tested, what happened, and where you
still need to investigate.

00:26.540 --> 00:27.750
Let's run a first test.

00:28.260 --> 00:29.900
The command-line tool needs Bun.

00:30.500 --> 00:33.790
With Bun installed, install the CLI, and
check its version.

00:34.460 --> 00:38.660
Next, configure a supported model provider
using your own credentials.

00:39.190 --> 00:41.000
Create a small YAML scenario.

00:41.540 --> 00:45.220
Give a case a prompt, then specify what
the response must contain.

00:45.770 --> 00:48.320
For this example, the expected value is
four.

00:48.930 --> 00:52.110
Run the scenario with ArtemisKit using the
command on screen.

00:52.770 --> 00:56.910
Here, we've connected a local model
through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.

00:57.420 --> 01:01.570
ArtemisKit records the response and the
check alongside the run details.

01:02.100 --> 01:03.550
This single case passed.

01:04.010 --> 01:08.200
That tells us only that this model, in
this run, met this one check.

01:08.790 --> 01:12.240
Now add a boundary case, change one thing,
and run it again.

01:12.500 --> 01:15.180
Discover more at artemiskit.dev.

01:15.620 --> 01:17.110
Your AI has a job.

01:17.480 --> 01:18.769
Test it for that job.